Wait, my toggle() is convoluted and buggy-looking. Simplify: toggle(force) { if (force !== undefined) return this.open = !!force... Actually standard pattern:

```js
toggle(force) {
  if (force !== undefined) { this.open = force; return this.open; }
  this.open = !this.open;
  return this.open;
}
```

Also #setOpen: when element not connected, attributeChangedCallback still fires? Yes, attributeChangedCallback fires even before connection for observed attributes (after upgrade). But I guard `this.isConnected` in attributeChangedCallback to avoid double dispatch during parsing? Actually the guard prevents dispatch/render when setting attribute while disconnected — that's bad: tests might set open before connecting. Better: always render + dispatch in attributeChangedCallback. The `oldValue !== newValue` guard handles redundant sets. But then in constructor/parse time initial attribute set fires callback once with oldValue null → fine, single dispatch. Let me simplify: attributeChangedCallback handles both dispatch and render unconditionally (with change guard). And click handler just toggles attribute.

Edge case: setting same value (e.g., setAttribute('open','') twice) — oldVal '' vs '' equal, no dispatch. Good.

Rewrite component more cleanly.

